{
  "teamId": 145,
  "year": 2021,
  "narrative": "The 2021 Chicago White Sox arrived with genuine expectations, and they delivered on them — 93 wins, an AL Central crown, and the franchise's best record in over a decade. From the very first weekend, José Abreu set the tone with a grand slam that announced this team meant business. April was a grind, but the Sox went 14-11 and showed the resilience that would define their summer. Adam Eaton's walk-off energy and Nick Madrigal's clutch doubles kept the ship steady even as the bullpen showed its first cracks.\n\nMay was arguably the season's finest month — a blistering 19-10 record that put the division on notice. The offense clicked, Liam Hendriks was mostly dominant, and the Sox looked every bit like a postseason-caliber club. June and July brought a more turbulent stretch, with Hendriks absorbing several gut-punch home runs that cost the team close games, yet Chicago kept winning at a 14-12 clip in both months. The defining image of July was Adam Engel's three-run blast flipping a deficit into a lead, and Eloy Jiménez returning from his torn pectoral to homer in his very first at-bat back — a storybook moment that electrified a clubhouse hungry for a push.\n\nAugust produced the season's single most dramatic swing: on August 12th, Giancarlo Stanton clubbed a Hendriks offering into the left-field seats to seemingly crush Chicago's hopes, only for Tim Anderson to answer moments later with a walk-off blast that registered as the highest-WPA play of the entire season. That kind of grit — absorbing a gut shot and swinging back — captured the White Sox at their best. The team finished 16-12 in August and 14-12 in September, inexorably grinding toward the division title and setting up an October showdown with the Houston Astros.\n\nThe ALDS told a different, painful story. In three games against the Astros, the White Sox managed only a brief glimpse of hope — Luis Robert's RBI single in Game 1 and Leury García's three-run homer in Game 3 — before Houston's relentless lineup tore through Chicago's pitching. Carlos Correa's clutch doubles in Games 1 and 3, Kyle Tucker's two-home-run performance in Game 2, and a parade of Astros crossing the plate against Crochet, Kimbrel, Cease, and Rodón sent the Sox home in a sweep. The dream season ended in bitter disappointment, but it also confirmed that the South Side's window was not just open — it was wide, and the future felt close enough to touch.",
  "chapters": [
